1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:09,008
Four hundred kilometres above us, Earth's gravity is still
eighty-nine percent as strong as it is here on the ground.

2
00:00:09,328 --> 00:00:12,123
Yet the astronauts on
the space station float.

3
00:00:12,443 --> 00:00:14,619
Their water floats in round drops.

4
00:00:14,939 --> 00:00:16,795
So where did gravity go?

5
00:00:17,115 --> 00:00:18,757
It did not go anywhere.

6
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:22,917
In five minutes, you will know
what is really happening up there.

7
00:00:24,137 --> 00:00:25,524
Start in a park.

8
00:00:25,844 --> 00:00:29,599
Throw a ball, and it curves down
and lands a few steps away.

9
00:00:29,919 --> 00:00:31,135
Throw harder.

10
00:00:31,455 --> 00:00:34,015
It still falls, but it lands further away.

11
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:35,807
The fall is the same.

12
00:00:36,127 --> 00:00:39,689
Only the distance it travels
before it lands has changed.

13
00:00:40,909 --> 00:00:45,240
Isaac Newton pictured this with
a cannon on a very tall mountain.

14
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:49,144
Fire the ball faster, and it
lands further round the Earth.

15
00:00:49,464 --> 00:00:52,557
Fire it fast enough, and
it never lands at all.

16
00:00:52,877 --> 00:00:58,488
The Earth is round, so the ground curves away
under the ball as fast as the ball falls.

17
00:00:58,808 --> 00:01:00,835
It falls all the way around.

18
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:05,656
Newton's picture was printed in 1728.

19
00:01:05,976 --> 00:01:08,813
That endless fall is
what we call an orbit.

20
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:15,132
Let us put real numbers on it, for one
second at four hundred kilometres up.

21
00:01:15,452 --> 00:01:20,551
In that second, the station moves seven
point six seven kilometres sideways.

22
00:01:20,871 --> 00:01:24,391
Gravity pulls it down by
about four point three metres.

23
00:01:24,711 --> 00:01:31,985
And over seven point six seven kilometres, the round
Earth curves away by about four point three metres too.

24
00:01:32,305 --> 00:01:34,567
So the station falls, and misses.

25
00:01:34,887 --> 00:01:36,657
Every second, all day.

26
00:01:37,877 --> 00:01:43,211
At four hundred kilometres, its pull is
eighty-nine percent of what you feel now.

27
00:01:43,531 --> 00:01:47,755
The station is only a little further
from Earth's centre than you are.

28
00:01:48,075 --> 00:01:55,221
Six thousand seven hundred and seventy-eight kilometres,
against six thousand three hundred and seventy-eight.

29
00:01:56,441 --> 00:01:58,724
So why do the astronauts float?

30
00:01:59,044 --> 00:02:00,751
Because they are falling too.

31
00:02:01,071 --> 00:02:06,553
The station, the crew, the pens and the
water all fall together, at the same rate.

32
00:02:06,873 --> 00:02:11,823
You feel this for a moment on a fairground
drop ride, when your stomach lifts.

33
00:02:12,143 --> 00:02:14,511
Astronauts feel it all day long.

34
00:02:15,731 --> 00:02:17,181
Now the key idea.

35
00:02:17,501 --> 00:02:20,061
The sideways speed is not a free choice.

36
00:02:20,381 --> 00:02:22,067
It is set by the height.

37
00:02:22,387 --> 00:02:25,928
Lower down, gravity pulls
harder, so you fall faster.

38
00:02:26,248 --> 00:02:29,576
To keep missing the ground,
you must move faster too.

39
00:02:29,896 --> 00:02:35,571
Higher up, gravity is weaker, so the right
speed is lower, and the trip around is longer.

40
00:02:36,791 --> 00:02:39,052
Here are the numbers from the lab.

41
00:02:39,372 --> 00:02:44,407
At two hundred and eighty kilometres,
one orbit takes ninety point one minutes.

42
00:02:44,727 --> 00:02:53,623
At four hundred kilometres, it takes ninety-two point six minutes,
at about twenty-seven thousand six hundred kilometres an hour.

43
00:02:53,943 --> 00:02:59,489
At two thousand kilometres, one lap stretches
to one hundred and twenty-seven minutes.

44
00:02:59,809 --> 00:03:04,951
NASA says each space station orbit
takes ninety to ninety-three minutes.

45
00:03:05,271 --> 00:03:11,905
In the lab, that is a height of about two hundred and
seventy-four to four hundred and twenty-one kilometres.

46
00:03:13,125 --> 00:03:15,856
Keep climbing, and each
orbit takes longer.

47
00:03:16,176 --> 00:03:25,733
At one special height, thirty-five thousand seven hundred and eighty-six
kilometres, one orbit takes twenty-three point nine three hours.

48
00:03:26,053 --> 00:03:30,533
That is how long Earth takes to spin
once, measured against the stars.

49
00:03:30,853 --> 00:03:34,203
From your roof, it seems
to hang still in one spot.

50
00:03:34,523 --> 00:03:37,488
That is why a TV dish never has to move.

51
00:03:38,708 --> 00:03:40,735
Here is the strangest part.

52
00:03:41,055 --> 00:03:45,321
Say your spacecraft is behind the
station, and you want to catch up.

53
00:03:45,641 --> 00:03:47,433
On a road, you would speed up.

54
00:03:47,753 --> 00:03:49,460
In orbit, that backfires.

55
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:53,812
Push forwards, and you rise to
a higher orbit, which is slower.

56
00:03:54,132 --> 00:03:55,839
You fall further behind.

57
00:03:57,059 --> 00:03:58,616
So you brake instead.

58
00:03:58,936 --> 00:04:04,227
You drop to a lower, faster orbit, gain on
the station, and then climb to meet it.

59
00:04:04,547 --> 00:04:10,669
In the lab, an orbit just ten kilometres lower
is about twelve seconds shorter, every lap.

60
00:04:11,889 --> 00:04:14,940
Here it is in the free
Rimi Lab Cosmos lab.

61
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:21,639
Set the height to four hundred kilometres, and
one orbit takes ninety-two point five six minutes.

62
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:26,801
Slide down to two hundred and eighty,
and it drops to ninety point one one.

63
00:04:27,121 --> 00:04:32,220
Slide up to two thousand, and it grows to
one hundred and twenty-seven point two.

64
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:35,168
Three things to remember.

65
00:04:35,488 --> 00:04:40,395
One, an orbit is falling sideways, fast
enough to keep missing the ground.

66
00:04:40,715 --> 00:04:45,344
Two, gravity is still there, but astronauts
fall together with their station.

67
00:04:45,664 --> 00:04:47,605
Three, height sets the speed.

68
00:04:47,925 --> 00:04:50,464
Lower is faster, and higher is slower.

69
00:04:51,684 --> 00:04:53,476
Tonight, look for the station.

70
00:04:53,796 --> 00:04:57,657
It looks like a bright star that
moves steadily across the sky.

71
00:04:57,977 --> 00:05:02,372
Try the orbit lab for free at
Rimi Lab Cosmos, link below.

72
00:05:02,692 --> 00:05:06,937
Next time, we go back to work,
with one more idea from business.

73
00:05:07,257 --> 00:05:08,388
See you then.

